WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.805
A transaction acts like a single move.

00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:07.610
We saw why we need that.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.415
Now the promise: four guarantees that spell ACID.

00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:13.220
Get all four, and a database holds real money.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.025
Drop one, and money goes missing.

00:00:16.025 --> 00:00:20.429
Take Alice's one purchase from last page, those three steps wrapped as a single transaction.

00:00:20.429 --> 00:00:24.833
It can end two ways.

00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:29.237
All three succeed, so the transaction commits and her seat is booked.

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:33.641
Or the card declines, so the whole transaction rolls back.

00:00:33.641 --> 00:00:38.045
No seat held, no charge, as if she never clicked.

00:00:38.045 --> 00:00:42.449
That all-or-nothing is the first guarantee, and four of them protect this purchase, even when a million people click buy at once.

00:00:42.449 --> 00:00:45.236
Here are all four, one color each.

00:00:45.236 --> 00:00:48.022
Atomicity.

00:00:48.022 --> 00:00:50.809
The bundle cannot be split.

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:53.596
Alice's card declines halfway, and the database pretends the whole attempt never happened.

00:00:53.596 --> 00:00:56.383
No ticket, no payment, no inventory touched.

00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:59.169
Consistency.

00:00:59.169 --> 00:01:01.956
The database always obeys its own rules.

00:01:01.956 --> 00:01:04.743
Bob's purchase moves the ticket count and the revenue together, as one unit.

00:01:04.743 --> 00:01:07.530
You never see one move without the other.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.316
Isolation.

00:01:10.316 --> 00:01:13.103
Concurrent transactions do not see each other's half-done work.

00:01:13.103 --> 00:01:15.890
Alice and Bob both grab the last ticket.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.677
Alice locks the row first.

00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:21.463
So what happens to Bob?

00:01:21.463 --> 00:01:24.250
Bob waits.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.037
No double sale.

00:01:27.037 --> 00:01:29.824
Durability.

00:01:29.824 --> 00:01:32.610
Once the database says committed, that answer is permanent.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.397
Bob sees his receipt, the server crashes a millisecond later, and after recovery his ticket is still there.

00:01:35.397 --> 00:01:39.069
Isolation deserves a closer look, with money.

00:01:39.069 --> 00:01:42.740
Month-end.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.412
Alice has two hundred dollars, Bob one hundred, three hundred in all.

00:01:46.412 --> 00:01:50.084
Two transactions fire at once: Alice transfers a hundred to Bob, and the bank adds six percent interest to every account.

00:01:50.084 --> 00:01:53.756
Without isolation, they interleave.

00:01:53.756 --> 00:01:57.427
The transfer credits Bob first, so Bob already shows two hundred.

00:01:57.427 --> 00:02:01.099
But Alice has not been debited yet, so she still shows two hundred.

00:02:01.099 --> 00:02:04.771
Now the interest job reads the balances.

00:02:04.771 --> 00:02:08.442
On which total does it pay?

00:02:08.442 --> 00:02:12.114
Four hundred dollars.

00:02:12.114 --> 00:02:15.786
Both read the same half-done state, the moving hundred counted in both accounts at once.

00:02:15.786 --> 00:02:19.458
So interest pays six percent on four hundred, not three.

00:02:19.458 --> 00:02:23.129
The books land at three hundred twenty four.

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:26.801
Six dollars created from nothing.

00:02:26.801 --> 00:02:30.473
Now turn isolation on.

00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:34.145
The interest job waits on the transfer's lock until it commits.

00:02:34.145 --> 00:02:37.816
Then it reads the settled balances, a clean three hundred, and pays exactly eighteen dollars.

00:02:37.816 --> 00:02:41.488
The books land at three hundred eighteen, not a cent invented.

00:02:41.488 --> 00:02:44.971
ACID is the contract that turns stored bytes into a system you trust with money.

00:02:44.971 --> 00:02:48.454
Next, all four at once in a real payment.

00:02:48.454 --> 00:02:51.937
Stripe moves a trillion dollars a year.
